# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two FILES, never a
# shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is defined in run_gates.sh.
# Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_call_edge_precision.py" "CALL EDGE PRECISION GATE: PASS" "call-edge precision: thin test-origin call edges are kept (recall-safe), not droppable in the extractor" "call-edge precision RECALL GUARD: a THIN TEST-ORIGIN import-unconfirmed single-definer \`calls\` edge (a test calling a common name -- route/open/process -- defined once in prod, no import) is the dominant CALL-graph cry-wolf class (measured: flask 151 / axios 170 / sinatra 165 / zustand 24 such edges). DROPPING it is NOT recall-safe -- those edges carry real co-change coupling (flask co-change recall 67->60, zustand 15->11 if dropped; 0 of the recall-bearing pairs are import-covered, so the call edge is the SOLE carrier). And the extractor CANNOT demote it to a lower-confidence kind: edge_kind is pinned to {contains,calls,imports,queries,alters,reads_config} by a CHECK constraint AND the gate-ingest WHERE-clause, so a 'calls_weak'/'unknown' kind is SILENTLY DROPPED at ingest = recall loss. The recall-safe demotion ('unknown'+dampened_with, never dropped) lives in the SQL engine (out_adj/in_adj/dampened) -- a symmetric test-as-caller guard, handed off, NOT forced into the extractor. This gate PINS that the thin test-origin call edge stays a normal \`calls\` edge (a future 'precision fix' that drops it FAILS here) AND that existing precision (import-confirmed kept, prod single-definer kept) is intact. Hermetic synthetic graph; content-free (edge kinds + dst names)" "call-edge precision: the thin test-origin call edge is recall-safely KEPT as \`calls\` (never dropped, never minted into a non-ingestible kind); the demotion is a SQL-engine change, not an extractor change; existing precision intact" 20
